 oneplus one over k  edges  but let me go to the faulty setting  and  let's see how we can change the  algorithm to make it fall tolerant this  algorithm was first proposed by um both  v net alexa  2018  and  it's the same as the algorithm described  we again process the edges in the  non-decreasing weight order  but the difference is here when we  consider an suv we need to check and see  if there's any fault set f of size at  most f  that basically forces us to  add this edge  so is there any faults at f  such that the distance after we remove  uh  the nodes in f  becomes larger than 2k minus 1 times the  weight of uv  if there exists a default set then we  add uh  huv to the spanner  so clearly um  we are adding  um more edges because this fault set is  um forcing us to add more edges  okay now this was a algorithm for  spanner so  what do we do to turn it into an  emulator algorithm  as discussed we should somehow utilize  the fact that emulators give us some  more flexibility because we can add new  edges somewhere in the algorithm  so  the other thing to remember is that uh  the weights of these edges that we add  that didn't exist in g can also be  updated after faults  and  um i'm going to give you an algorithm  for five emulator because the general  algorithm is much more involved and it  requires new definitions so  i won't get into it  and the idea here is that whenever we  add a spanner edge uv  in the greedy algorithm  we also sample a set of emulator edges  like the edges that  are potentially not there  corresponding to three pads that have uv  as a  middle edge and in a weighted case uv  has to be the heaviest edge on this path  but  don't worry about those technicalities  um so here's  how the algorithm looks like  same as before we start with  an empty  subgraph  and  we have  a  parameter d that depends on f  and now for  again we go through edges  in on decreasing weight order and we see  if  for an edge uv there exists a fault set  that forces us to add this edge  um  and  if we add an edge uv  then in addition to uv we add a  sampled set of emulator edges  as follows we look at neighbors  of u  for example s  and  neighbors of v for example t we consider  such st  pairs  and  we add an emulator edge between s and t  with probability one over d squared  so this is really what is different in  the emulator algorithm with the spanner  algorithm  and  you will see that it will be helpful to  consider two type of edges in each the  spanner edges which are the edges that  already exist in g and the emulator  edges which are these  new sampled edges  they kind of have different behaviors in  our algorithm as we will see  so um  this algorithm does something  counterintuitive  because in a sense we're saying um  whenever we're adding  spanner edges uv  we're  going to add emulator edges in the same  vicinity  but intuitively  this helps us because  these additional emulator edges that  we're adding kind of help us reduce  adding more spanner edges later  and also  in  uh  in in case of f fault tolerant spanners  what we really want is  uh  to make sure that we aren't adding more  than roughly f pads between any pair of  nodes like you want to sparse a  graph that's what we should do we  shouldn't add more than  roughly f pads between any pairs  and  somehow  these emulator edges  along these three pads  will help us not add  too many  edges  so  very briefly correctness is again  easy  i won't get into it  the size analysis is more complicated  and  the idea is that  remember  we are  adding this emulator edges based on the  sampling probability  and somehow  um  the way this  sampling works is that  we can  ensure that for any pair of nodes s and  t  there's a  roughly  um  f  three pads that we are sampling from  and  uh  we can  use this process to charge  the emulator edges added to the spanner  i just added so in our analysis  uh  we can  bound the number of emulator edges that  are being added based on the number of  spanner edges that are being added  and then the path counting um  like we count the number of three paths  that we could sample from  and  somehow we can  use this to bound the number of spanner  edges and  hence  the number of corresponding emulator  edges  so  of course we  wanted to also give an algorithm for  general k i won't get into details of it  because it's more complicated we have to  define certain  set of paths  with certain properties  and then  the sampling proper  probability will depend on the length of  these pads  um  i  skip this  algorithm for this talk  but let me  finish by  some remarks on lower bounds and  polynomial time construction  so  i briefly mentioned the erdos gert  conjecture  which was  saying for any  integer k um there are graphs  with  girth at least two k plus two  and uh  at least n to the one plus one over k  edges  so this is a well-known conjecture  um and it's proven for some values of k  but using this conjecture we turn it  into  a lower bound for  constructing k emulators  2k minus 1 emulators  and basically  show that  we need at least roughly this many edges  and  the  instance is not very important but  at a high level we start with a girth  conjecture graph and we do some  transformation in particular we replace  each edge with a bipartite graph of  roughly size square root f  and then uh we have to reason that um  emulator edges can only replace a  constant number of spanner edges  uh so more or less the overall number of  edges we have to keep in this instance  is around  the original number of edges  which was  which gives us this point  finally the other thing to note is that  the algorithm i gave you takes  exponential time because for each edge  uv  we need to check  into the f possible fault sets  when we decide whether to add the edge  and  this is exponential in f so this is not  desirable  and to make this algorithm polynomial  time we use ideas  by  linux and robo which basically  lead to a polynomial time algorithm at  some additional factors of k in the size  and at a very high level the idea is to  replace this  entity f time procedure with  an algorithm that approximates the  smallest faults that f that  causes the distances to  go above the threshold  um and this approximation  algorithm is what  adds some additional factor in size  okay let me conclude  i described an algorithm for  constructing vertex fault tolerant  emulators  and also  uh  mentioned a matching lower bound with  respect to faults as a  file size f  but  this lower bound  matches the upper bound only when k is  odd  so the first open question  um is  can we close this gap for even k  and as i mentioned before this is also  on resolve for spanners um but for edge  fault tolerant case so maybe we should  answer it there first  the other natural question is  what happens if we  consider  edge failures instead of vertex failures  can we  get a better  size dependence on the number of  failures in that case  and  the other question is can we improve the  dependence on k in the size  and finally another question is  what can we do uh  for  other related objects like can we use  similar ideas to construct  say a fault tolerant distance oracles  or  other objects that are related  and with that  i finish this talk and thank you please  contact me with any questions you might  have
 

